The next day, his stall was full. Eighty merchants had come—successful partners from across the years. They'd brought gifts, shared memories, wept together. The two betrayers stood apart, witnessing.

"See?" the trader whispered to them. "You two asked if I felt foolish. But look at this room. These friendships, this community—they exist because I didn't let you seven betrayers teach me to close off."

He gestured to the crowd. "Each time one of you cheated me, I had a choice: protect myself by trusting no one, or protect my values by still trusting new someones. I chose my values. And look what bloomed."

To the recent betrayer, he said gently, "You hurt me, yes. But you didn't define me. Only I define me. That's my freedom."

The room full of loving friends answered the question better than words ever could.
